  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWe expect to see an increase in the coverage of precipitation \nWednesday night beginning out west across the Rio Grande plains as\na Pacific front slowly moves in from the west. Plenty of moisture\nwill be in place ahead of this feature as precipitable water \nvalues likely climb to around 2\" for most areas. WPC continues to \nshow a level 2 of 4 risk for flooding generally along and north of\na Del Rio to Austin line, with a level 1 of 4 risk farther east \nthrough most of the I-35 corridor. \n\nOn Thursday, rain chances will increase significantly across the \nregion as the Pacific front and upper level trough axis approach \nfrom the west. The medium range models hint at a southwest to \nnortheast oriented line of convection developing early across the \nsouthern Edwards Plateau and northern Hill Country, with convection \nexpected to expand slowly eastward during the day as peak heating is \nrealized. With the Pacific front, well above normal moisture and \nupper level trough axis, heavy rainfall is likely to develop across \na good portion of the region Thursday and into Thursday night. The \nWPC continues to show a level 2 of 4 risk in place across south \ncentral Texas, with a level 3 of 4 over the Hill Country and I-35 \ncorridor. \n\nRain chances remain high into Friday, but much will depend on the \nspeed of the Pacific cold front. The tail end of the NAM shows the \nfront pushing southward into the coastal plains early Friday \nmorning, which would help shift the focus for heavy rainfall a \nlittle farther to the east. The ECMWF and GFS ensembles as well as \ntheir AI runs are starting to show some of the heavier precipitation \namounts across portions of the Rio Grande plains between Del Rio and \nSan Antonio, northward into the Hill Country. We are still early in \nthis event and as the hi-res members start to pick up on this, \nadditional changes to the forecast are likely. We will certainly \nneed to watch Thursday, Thursday night and even into early Friday \nmorning as models are favoring this time period for the higher \nrainfall amounts. We could see some widespread rainfall amounts of 2 \nto 4 inches with local amounts up to 8 inches on Thursday into \nFriday. \n\nFor the upcoming weekend, some remnant upper level energy may hang \nback to our west as 'Rachel' continues in the eastern Pacific. While \nour western areas may see slightly lower moisture levels, we will \nstill have plenty of moisture to work with farther east into the I-\n35 corridor and coastal plains. Rain chances will remain in the \nforecast for the weekend into Monday. With good precipitation \nchances, northerly winds in the lower levels and cloud cover, highs \nwill generally range from the mid 70s to the lower 80s along with \novernight lows in the 60s.",
